IO::K8s::List's item_class is the only class-name-taking parameter in the
distribution that does not go through expand_class(), and when it is given
anything but a fully-qualified name the object half-works: kind() answers,
api_version() does not, and TO_JSON emits a manifest with a kind and no
apiVersion -- which the API server rejects.

Measured on 4887f658:

  item_class                      kind()      api_version()
  'Pod'                           PodList     undef
  'Api::Core::V1::Pod'            PodList     undef
  'IO::K8s::Api::Core::V1::Pod'   PodList     v1
  '+My::Thing'                    ThingList   undef

The asymmetry is structural, not accidental: kind() derives from the class
NAME by regex (List.pm:110-121), while api_version() asks the CLASS
(List.pm:80-88, `require_module` then `$class->api_version`, undef on any
failure -- karr #22). So a name that is not loadable still produces a Kind.
The '+' prefix, documented everywhere else in the distribution as "this is a
full class name", is not understood here either: it is not stripped, so
require_module('+My::Thing') fails while the regex happily reads the last
segment.

TO_JSON (List.pm:139-141) gates each key on its own accessor, so the result
is a payload with kind: PodList and no apiVersion: rather than an error.
That is the karr #39/#40 fail-closed rule not applied to this class.

NOT hit by Kubernetes::REST on its normal path -- verified against the
installed 1.107: list() calls expand_class() before _inflate_list(), so
item_class arrives fully qualified. Its public inflate_list() passthrough
(REST.pm:552) takes whatever the caller supplies, and a standalone consumer
constructing IO::K8s::List directly has nothing telling them the short names
that work everywhere else do not work here. The attr POD (List.pm:63-66)
just says "The class of items in the list".

WHAT TO DO -- decide the contract
  (a) Resolve it: run item_class through expand_class(). Needs an IO::K8s
      instance, which IO::K8s::List does not have and deliberately does not
      take (it is plain Moo with no dependency on the core object). Adding
      one is a real design change -- check whether a class-method
      expand_class call is enough (`IO::K8s->expand_class($name)` works
      against %DEFAULT_RESOURCE_MAP but sees no resource_map and no
      class_namespaces, so a consumer's own CRD would resolve differently
      here than everywhere else -- that inconsistency may be worse than the
      current state).
  (b) Keep it fully-qualified-only, but make it fail closed and say so:
      strip a leading '+', and make kind() refuse to answer for an
      item_class api_version() could not resolve, so the pair is emitted
      together or not at all. Plus a sentence in the attr POD.

(b) is the smaller change and matches what the class already is. (a) buys
consistency at the cost of giving List a dependency on the core object.
Decide, do not blend.

Whatever is chosen, the invariant to establish: IO::K8s::List never
serializes a kind without an apiVersion.

Test: t/44_list_api_version.t already owns exactly these claims -- it pins
the loadable case, the unloadable case (item_class 'Bauble'), and both
kind() paths from karr #41. Extend it there; the short-name and '+' cases
are simply missing from its matrix.

